= Voltage =

== Overview ==
'''Voltage''' is a difference in electric potential between two points. It describes the change in electric potential energy per unit charge between those locations and is measured in volts (V), where one volt is one joule per coulomb.

== Definition ==
When the potential energy of a test charge changes by ΔU as it moves between two points, the corresponding electric potential difference is ΔV = ΔU/q under the electrostatic definition, where q is the charge. A voltage is therefore a relation between locations or circuit nodes rather than a quantity that a single isolated point possesses without a reference.

== Voltage versus current ==
[[Related to::Electric current|Electric current]] is the rate at which electric charge crosses a chosen surface. A source may maintain a potential difference across two terminals even with no connected load and nearly no steady current. The actual current in a completed circuit depends on its components and source characteristics.

== Resistors and power ==
For an approximately ohmic [[Related to::Resistor|resistor]] under specified conditions, V = I R relates voltage, current and resistance. Electrical power transfer at a two-terminal element can be expressed as P = V I with the appropriate reference directions and sign convention. Voltage by itself is not the power or total stored energy available from a device.

== Examples ==
A battery's nominal terminal voltage states a potential difference; it does not alone specify capacity, available energy or how fast current can safely be delivered. Voltage may vary with load, state of charge and temperature.

== Limitations ==
The usual scalar-potential description works particularly well for electrostatics and lumped-circuit approximations. Time-varying magnetic fields and distributed systems require more careful electromagnetic treatment. Measurement requires a defined pair of points, instrument characteristics and conditions.

